  "answer": "The goal was not met: three settings met the confirmation bar (S26, S7, S9), short of the five asked for. None of the three is a clean case of the policy rear-ending a slowing lead car. On 19f339ba (S26) every run failed. The policy braked hard on an open lane and drifted left into a car in the next lane, so these were side collisions, not rear-ends. On 096988dd (S7) every run failed, but only at 350 ms planner delay, which is above the brief's 200 ms cap. Every one of those failures was flagged as a possible simulator artifact, and the ego steered left against a RIGHT route command into a neighbouring car. On 09a95ffa (S9: lead 1.5 s earlier at 0.5x speed, no delay) every run was a front collision with the lead. Each came 0.2 s after the policy took control, while the ego was still on the replayed human path, so triage does not blame the policy. These are small numbers of repeats, and the failure ranges are wide. The study gives no confirmed case where VaVAM itself fails to brake for a lead car within the brief's limits.",
